= Momentum =

== Overview ==
'''Linear momentum''' is a vector quantity equal to an object's or system's mass multiplied by its velocity.

== Definition ==
For constant mass, linear momentum is written p = mv. Its direction is the same as the velocity, and its SI unit is kg·m/s.

== Scope & importance ==
Momentum provides a powerful description of motion because total momentum is conserved in an isolated system. The momentum form of Newton's second law relates net external force to the rate of change of momentum.

== Core concepts ==
Momentum depends on both mass and velocity. Impulse is the change in momentum produced by a force acting over time. Conservation of momentum is especially useful for analyzing collisions and interactions.

== Examples ==
A slowly moving massive object can have more momentum than a rapidly moving lightweight object. In a collision, the total momentum of an isolated system is the same before and after the interaction even though individual objects change momentum.

== Applications / implications ==
Momentum is used throughout mechanics, collision analysis, rocketry, particle physics, fluid dynamics, biomechanics, and engineering.

== Epistemic notes ==
The elementary expression p = mv is the classical non-relativistic form. Relativistic mechanics modifies the relation between momentum, mass, and velocity at speeds where relativistic effects matter.

== Momentum as a system quantity ==
For a body of constant mass in classical mechanics, momentum is the vector p = m v. The net external force is the time rate of change of momentum, F_net = dp/dt. Its integral over a time interval is the impulse, equal to the change in momentum. This form is useful even when force changes substantially during a collision.

For a system of bodies, total momentum is the vector sum of their momenta. Conservation requires the '''net external impulse''' over the interval to be zero or negligible, not merely that individual objects exert equal and opposite contact forces.

== The importance of system boundaries ==
[[Depends on::System boundary|Choosing the system boundary]] establishes which forces count as internal and which are external. For example, the two colliding carts in a nearly frictionless track may exchange momentum internally; analyzing only one cart requires accounting for the interaction force exerted by the other.

== Worked conceptual example ==
Two identical carts approaching each other with equal speeds have equal and opposite momenta. Their combined momentum is zero both before and after an isolated collision, whether they rebound or stick together. Equal total momentum does not entail equal kinetic energy before and after: inelastic collisions may transform some kinetic energy into internal energy and deformation.

== Scope of the classical law ==
The expression p = mv is a non-relativistic approximation. High-speed particles require relativistic momentum; for a closed physical system, appropriate relativistic energy–momentum conservation remains fundamental.

